{"name":"Mustard Green","hex":"#a8b504","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mustard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of certain species of the genus Brassica, or of related genera (especially Sinapis alba, in the family Brassicaceae, with yellow flowers, and linear seed pods).","Powder or paste made from seeds of the mustard plant, and used as a condiment or a spice."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark yellow colour.","Very good, or very skilled."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mustard"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brassica juncea","blocks":[{"text":"Brassica juncea, commonly mustard greens, brown mustard, Chinese mustard, Indian mustard, Japanese mustard, Korean green mustard, leaf mustard, Oriental mustard and vegetable mustard, is a species of mustard plant."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Brassica_juncea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-168.jpg/330px-Brassica_juncea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-168.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brassica_juncea","wikidata":"Q504781"},{"title":"Mustard (condiment)","blocks":[{"text":"Mustard is a condiment made from the seeds of a mustard plant, which may be the white/yellow mustard, brown mustard, or black mustard."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Mustard.JPG/330px-Mustard.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_(condiment)","wikidata":"Q131748"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Mustard (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Mustard is a dull/dark yellow color that resembles culinary mustard. It is similar to the color Flax."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_(color)","wikidata":"Q1169566"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Mustardgreensraw.jpg/500px-Mustardgreensraw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Mustardgreensraw.jpg/500px-Mustardgreensraw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMustardgreensraw.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5